Here are concise, TRM-ready answers for your Storybook.js request:

Who will benefit from the software?
VA front-end developers, UI/UX designers, and product teams who build and maintain VA web applications. Specifically, teams working on VA LEAF and design system documentation will benefit from a centralized, interactive component library.

Describe the result of this software:
A living, interactive UI component documentation site that allows developers and designers to browse, test, and reference reusable front-end components in isolation — improving consistency and development efficiency across VA web products.

What are the key benefits of this software:

Centralizes UI component documentation in one accessible location
Enables isolated component development and testing without a full application environment
Reduces redundant development effort by promoting component reuse
Improves design-development collaboration through visual, interactive references
Already proven in use across VA (design.va.gov/storybook)

Provide a summary of your current situation or system:
There is currently no TRM-approved tool for interactive UI component documentation or design system cataloging within our team's workflow. Component documentation is either absent or maintained through static, non-interactive methods.

Describe how this software will be better than your current situation:
There is no current solution or similar software that performs this function in the TRM. Storybook.js fills a gap by providing a dedicated, interactive environment for documenting and validating UI components that no currently approved tool addresses.

Describe the features that are IN scope (what you need from OIT):
Approval for use and deployment of Storybook.js within a RHEL environment to support VA LEAF design system documentation. This includes OIT authorization to run the Storybook Node.js build process and serve static output on approved VA infrastructure.

Describe the features that are NOT in scope (what you will do yourself):
The team will independently handle deployment of Storybook.js on Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL), including installation, configuration, build pipeline setup, and ongoing maintenance of the Storybook instance.

Describe the non-functional requirements:

Compatibility: Must run on RHEL; built on Node.js, which is TRM-approved
Security: Operates as a static site generator with no database or user authentication surface; minimal attack vector
Availability: Intended for internal developer/documentation use; standard availability requirements apply
Maintainability: Open-source with active community support and regular versioning
Performance: Lightweight static output suitable for internal hosting
Accessibility: Supports documentation of Section 508-compliant components in line with VA standards

